WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Welcome to the closet of a woman in her thirties:
six black tops, three versions of the same hopeful outfit,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:20.000
and one dress waiting for an event that has not happened since 2019.
Today we are building a capsule wardrobe for real life—not a beige personality transplant.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:32.000
These are not boring clothes. These are evidence.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Start with the places your clothes need to take you during a normal week.
Look at the pieces you actually reach for, and make a short repeat list before you buy anything.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:30.000
[Visual demonstration: weekly-use audit and repeat list]

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Create five sections: everyday, work, movement, social, and weather or travel.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:05.000
The number of pieces depends on your lifestyle and climate.
There is no universal formula.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:03:00.000
[Visual demonstration: clothing grouped by role]

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Before buying, ask three questions.
Can it make three outfits with what I already own?

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Does it serve a real calendar situation?
And would I choose it on a tired Tuesday?

00:03:38.000 --> 00:04:15.000
If the answer is no to two of these, leave it in the shop.
Your bank account is allowed to have boundaries too.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Show how one accessory changes two existing outfits.
Choose by use first: comfort, durability, storage, weather,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:05:00.000
and how often the item will leave the house.
Avoid must-have language.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:22.000
I would wait a week before buying anything,
photograph the outfits I actually wear,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:40.000
and purchase only the missing role.
The goal is fewer decisions, not a closet that wins an argument online.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:06:00.000
This video may include affiliate links. If you shop through them,
we may earn a commission at no extra cost.
The links are in the description, and none of them are a commandment.
If an item does not earn its place, skip it and save your money.
